[req] tool_call  call_165 subagent({"agent":"librarian","background":true,"description":"Research CLM repo + Takahe serving","model":"dspark/qwen3.8-flash-next","prompt":"Investigate Contrastive Language Models (CLM) from the GitHub repo https://github.com/Contrastive-LM/CLM and how it is served/deployed. Read the actual repo README/docs/source — do NOT rely on assumptions. I need authoritative, cited answers to:\n\n1. What CLM is (model family, architecture) and what its inference/serving interface actually exposes. Specifically: does it provide an **OpenAI-compatible** endpoint (`/v1/chat/completions` and/or `/v1/completions`), or a different native API? Show the exact request/response shapes and how to call it.\n\n2. Output-shape details that matter to a consumer: is there streaming (SSE / `delta`)? Is token `usage` reported? Is there a chat/instruct format or only base text completion? Any structured/JSON output support? Tokenization notes?\n\n3. How CLM is meant to be deployed/served (which serving stack: vLLM, their own server, container image, ports, env vars, API key/auth if any).\n\n4. \"Takahe\": investigate whether Takahe (search the web / Microsoft Research) is a serving/runtime platform for models like CLM, and how CLM would be launched on Takahe — what base URL, port, and auth a client would use. If CLM-on-Takahe is not documented, say so explicitly and give the closest documented serving path.\n\nReport: (a) CLM↔OpenAI-compatibility verdict with exact endpoint evidence, (b) any incompatibilities a client expecting chat-completions + streaming-delta + usage would hit, (c) concrete deploy steps for CLM (and CLM-on-Takahe if findable), (d) the client base_url/endpoint it would expose. Cite every claim with the doc/repo/URL it came from."})
[req] tool_call  call_166 subagent({"agent":"explorer","background":true,"description":"Map VeloBench helper integration","model":"dspark/qwen3.8-flash-next","prompt":"In the VeloBench Rust+Angular repo (working dir /Users/sean/repo.opensource/VeloBench), map the complete \"Helper Model\" integration surface so we can plan replacing it with a different model backend. Give me a precise file/line map, not full file dumps. Cover:\n\n1. `src/settings.rs`: the `HelperConfig` struct (every field — base_url, key, model id, concurrency, anything else), where it's normalized/validated, and its default/serde behavior.\n\n2. `src/classify.rs`: how `resolve_helper` builds the request, the exact HTTP call it makes (endpoint path, headers, body shape, auth), what output contract it expects back (the classification JSON — dominant regime + ordered regime list), the timeout, and error handling. Quote the request-building and response-parsing functions with line numbers.\n\n3. `src/analyze.rs`: how the helper is used for post-hoc session meta-analysis — what prompts/contracts it sends, and how its results feed analysis. Note any assumptions about streaming, usage tokens, or chat format.\n\n4. Where the helper endpoint is actually invoked at runtime (which handlers/routes in `src/server.rs` trigger classify/analyze), and whether there's a \"free tier\" / non-helper fallback path for classification (search for \"free tier\", \"free_tier\", fallback).\n\n5. Frontend: the Settings \"Helper Model\" tab component under `frontend/src/app/` — where HelperConfig is edited/displayed and which service/API saves it.\n\n6. Note every place that assumes an **OpenAI-compatible chat-completions** API so we know what a CLM adapter must satisfy or replace.\n\nDeliverable: a compact map (file:line → responsibility) plus the exact request/response contracts of classify and analyze, and a list of all OpenAI-compat assumptions. This is read-only discovery."})
